<% content_for :title, "Accessibility — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "What Shadwire components handle for accessibility, what is still up to you, and how the project tests it." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Accessibility",
        description: "The components follow the WAI-ARIA APG patterns and use native HTML elements wherever they can. Only a few things are left for you to do." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("What comes built in") %>

    <%= render "docs/table",
          headers: [ "Area", "What the component already does" ],
          rows: [
            [ "Native element first", safe_join([ "Dialog, Alert Dialog, Sheet and Drawer use the native ", docs_code("<dialog>"), ": focus trapping, ", docs_code("Esc"), " and focus restoration come from the browser. Checkbox, Radio Group and Switch are styled native inputs." ]) ],
            [ "Roles and states", safe_join([ "Tabs exposes ", docs_code("tablist"), "/", docs_code("tab"), "/", docs_code("tabpanel"), "; Select is an APG combobox with ", docs_code("aria-activedescendant"), "; Toggle uses ", docs_code("aria-pressed"), "; Progress exposes ", docs_code("aria-valuenow"), "." ]) ],
            [ "Keyboard", "Arrow keys navigate menus, tabs, select and toggle group; typeahead matches by prefix in menus and select; Esc closes overlays." ],
            [ "Visible focus", safe_join([ "Every interactive control shows a focus ring in the ", docs_code("ring"), " colour when it has ", docs_code(":focus-visible"), "." ]) ],
            [ "Decoration is hidden", safe_join([ "Icons render ", docs_code('aria-hidden="true"'), " by default; decorative separators leave the accessibility tree." ]) ]
          ] %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      In the Shadwire repository, every component has render tests, and an accessibility test
      goes through the site's pages checking structure, labels and navigation. Browser tests
      run in headless Chrome on every change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("What's left to you") %>

    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Naming overlays") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          <%= docs_code("<dialog>") %> takes care of focus and the keyboard, but it cannot come
          up with a name. Every dialog, sheet and drawer needs its title component; add
          <%= docs_code('class: "sr-only"') %> when the design has no visible title.
        </p>
        <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:overlay]) %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Naming icons that carry meaning") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          An icon next to text is decoration and should stay hidden. An icon on its own is the
          label, so pass <%= docs_code("label:") %> or label the control around it. There is more
          on the <%= link_to "Icons", docs_icons_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %> page.
        </p>
        <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:icon_label]) %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Tying label to control") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          Since there is no form builder, you set <%= docs_code("for:") %> and
          <%= docs_code("id:") %> yourself. <%= docs_code("ui_field") %> lays out the row, but it
          cannot guess the control's id.
          See <%= link_to "Forms", docs_forms_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Contrast after retheming") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          The default colours have enough contrast. If you change them, check yours: change
          <%= docs_code("<surface>") %> and <%= docs_code("<surface>-foreground") %> together, and
          test both modes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Heading hierarchy") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          <%= docs_code("ui_card_title") %> and <%= docs_code("ui_dialog_title") %> set how a title
          looks, not its heading level. If the page needs a heading structure, add the
          <%= docs_code("h1") %>–<%= docs_code("h3") %> elements yourself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Testing in your app") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The installed components are part of your code, so you test them like any other
      ViewComponent. Make sure your tests check the accessible name, since it depends on what
      you pass in:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:test], language: :ruby) %>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "Interactive components need a browser" } do %>
      Focus, keyboard handling and <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> behaviour can only be tested in a
      system test. A render test checks the markup, not whether <code>Esc</code> closes the
      dialog.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Found a problem?") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      If a component has an accessibility problem, that is a bug in Shadwire, not in your app.
      Please <%= link_to "open an issue", "https://github.com/edumoraes/shadwire/issues/new", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
      Meanwhile the file is yours, so you can fix it locally; run
      <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire diff") %> before your next update so you don't lose the fix.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
